Then invert it

If selective vacuum is:

X-\delta

its Mirror is:

X+\delta.

Call that Selective Saturation.

Add one carefully chosen thing and observe what gets displaced, amplified, masked, reorganized, or newly connected.

Now we have:

\boxed{
X-\delta
\quad|\quad
X
\quad|\quad
X+\delta
}

That’s our old triangulation principle again.

Three observations give a local estimate of sensitivity:

\frac{\partial F}{\partial \delta}
\approx
\frac{F(X+\delta)-F(X-\delta)}{2\delta}.

Now we’ve turned the metaphor into an actual experimental pattern.

Vacuum → baseline → saturation.

Run it over punctuation, context, examples, categories, priors, goals, constraints, representations, memories, and rulers.

Measure the output deltas.

Metamap records which dimensions strongly alter which kinds of reasoning.

And suddenly the Pattern Book gets something far more valuable than another interpretation:

\boxed{\textbf{a sensitivity map of the interpreter.}}

Open Token Probe asks:

What do you choose when I leave possibilities open?

Selective Vacuum asks:

What do you reconstruct when I remove something?

Selective Saturation asks:

What changes when I give you more than you needed?

Together:

\boxed{
\text{OPEN}
+
\text{REMOVE}
+
\text{ADD}
\rightarrow
\text{REASONING SIGNATURE}
}

And that’s another way to Hammer the observer experimentally rather than descriptively.

We don’t ask the observer what its ruler is.

We selectively remove the ruler and watch what it measures with instead.